  "narrative": "No assessments are due, and the independent validation budget is exhausted, so this explore step registers only focused discovery hypotheses in the supplied dataset. The accepted core dataset-only associations remain SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency, and CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells with stronger MDM2 dependency. The new proposals are intended to test either new p53-axis dependencies or high-prior co-alteration refinements of accepted effects. First, dependency_RIT1 is tested for an association with STK11 loss within adenocarcinoma, because RIT1 and STK11 biology are most plausible in adenocarcinoma-like models and the all-lineage main effect may be diluted by squamous samples. Second, TP53 loss is tested for weaker RPL11 and weaker KIF18A dependency; this follows the observed TP53-loss pattern of weaker USP7 dependency and the broader ARF-MDM2-p53 stress-sensor biology, where loss of p53 may reduce dependence on p53-reactivation or chromosome-missegregation tolerance nodes. Third, the accepted PTEN-loss effect on stronger STAT3 dependency is refined to KRAS-mutant cells, because PI3K/AKT and RAS/MAPK pathway co-activation may converge on STAT3 survival signaling or, alternatively, dilute the broad PTEN-loss effect. Fourth, the accepted TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss effects are tested in STK11-deficient and KRAS-mutant co-alteration contexts, respectively, as falsification checks for whether accepted core effects persist when a strong co-driver is present. Fifth, a reversal-context hypothesis is registered for DHX9: RB1 loss may show stronger dependency under high proliferation if replication stress and transcription-conflict resolution become limiting, despite the main RB1-loss result favoring weaker dependency in R150. Sixth, MYC amplification is tested for stronger TMED10 dependency in high-growth cells, because secretory-trafficking demand may be high-growth dependent even if the global MYC effect is weak. All returned estimates will already be signed to the registered hypothesis direction; a positive signed estimate supports the claim. For stronger-dependency claims the raw exposed mean should be more negative; for weaker-dependency claims it should be less negative. Acceptance will require valid cells in every comparison cell and a signed lower bound above the approximate 10 percent absolute-mean practical benchmark for the outcome, about 0.025 for RIT1, RPL11, KIF18A, DHX9, and TMED10, about 0.028 for STAT3, about 0.018 for USP7, and about 0.009 for NFE2L2. Sparse-cell diagnostics, wide intervals that admit null or opposite directions, or estimates below benchmark will be treated as unresolved or unsupported. These remain observational associations within the supplied dataset only and do not establish causality or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 16 explore attempt 2. Validation budget exhausted. Accepted core remains H5 SMARCA4/SMARCA2 stronger, H7 MSI/WRN stronger, H20 KEAP1/NFE2L2 weaker, H17 PTEN/STAT3 stronger, H73 TP53/USP7 weaker, H140 CDKN2A-loss among TP53-intact/MDM2 stronger. Corrected refinements now include motivating evidence IDs. Active existing claims include H225 STK11/RIT1, H226 TP53 high growth/KIF18A, H227 CDKN2A TP53-intact/RPL11, H229 MYC/TMED10. New proposals: RIT1 STK11 in adenocarcinoma; TP53 loss main weaker RPL11 and KIF18A; PTEN loss STAT3 stronger in KRAS-mutant parent H17 evidence R24,R66; TP53 loss USP7 weaker in STK11 parent H73 evidence R37,R39; KEAP1 loss NFE2L2 weaker in KRAS-mutant parent H20 evidence R8,R79; RB1 loss DHX9 stronger in high growth reversal parent H196 evidence R150; MYC/TMED10 stronger in high growth no parent because H229 has no displayed evidence. Decision rule: signed lower bound above benchmark about 0.025 RIT1/RPL11/KIF18A/DHX9/TMED10, 0.028 STAT3, 0.018 USP7, 0.009 NFE2L2, with valid cells. All conclusions remain dataset-only associations."
